artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $F$ be a field and let $\mathrm{Br}(F)$ denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of finite-dimensional central simple $F$-algebras under the tensor product, equivalently $H^2(\mathrm{Gal}(F^{\mathrm{sep}}/F), (F^{\mathrm{sep}})^\times)$. For a prime $p$, let $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ denote the $p$-primary component of $\mathrm{Br}(F)$, that is, the subgroup of elements whose order is a power of $p$. A subgroup $D \subseteq (\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ is called divisible if for every $x \in D$ and every positive integer $n$ there exists $y \in D$ with $ny = x$. An abelian $p$-group is called elementary abelian if every nonzero element has order $p$.

Prove or disprove: for every field $F$ and every prime $p$, at least one of the following three conditions holds:
(a) $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p = 0$;
(b) $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_p$ contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) $p = 2$ and $(\mathrm{Br}\, F)_2$ is an elementary abelian $2$-group.

